---
source: crates/lsw-cli/src/cli.rs
expression: "Cli::command().render_help().to_string()"
---
Linux Subsystem for Windows Development

Usage: lsw [OPTIONS] <COMMAND>

Commands:
  setup         Detect the project, create a default environment, and wire lsw.toml
  init          Scaffold a new project (lsw.toml + `CMake` template)
  env           Manage isolated Windows-target environments
  use           Select the active environment for this project
  build         Build Windows artifacts using native Linux tools
  run           Run an executable (PE via the Windows runtime, ELF natively)
  exec          Run a command in an explicit execution domain
  test          Build, then run tests under the local compatibility runtime
  check         Validate the project: build, wine execution, deps, hardening
  verify        Build, then verify artifacts on a real Windows host
  shell         Interactive shell: Linux with Windows-target env, or cmd.exe
  inspect       Inspect a PE binary: format, architecture, subsystem, imports
  crash         Decode a Windows crash dump: exception, faulting module, address
  audit         Audit a PE's security hardening (ASLR, DEP, CFG, `SafeSEH`, signing)
  exports       List the exported symbols of a PE (mirror of imports)
  sbom          Generate a `CycloneDX` SBOM for a PE (imports + toolchain provenance)
  diff          Diff two PEs by imports, exports, sections, and size
  size          Break down a PE's size by section bucket (code, data, resources, ...)
  strings       Extract printable ASCII and UTF-16 strings from a file
  deps          Inspect a PE's dependencies
  ci            Generate CI configuration
  config        Validate lsw.toml settings
  sign          Authenticode-sign a PE (self-signed by default, or a real PFX cert)
  path          Translate paths between Linux and Windows views
  registry      Read and write the environment's isolated registry
  debug         Debug a Windows binary with winedbg (or its gdb proxy)
  dap           Run a Debug Adapter Protocol server over stdio for IDEs
  compat        Measured compatibility report (imports + runtime trace)
  compat-query  Query the persistent compatibility database
  trace         Trace a Windows binary's DLL loads and API calls under the runtime
  package       Build and assemble a distributable package
  ps            List the environment's Windows/runtime processes
  kill          Terminate an environment process (or all with --all)
  service       Manage Windows services inside the environment
  rust          First-class Rust support
  dotnet        C#/.NET support (console, self-contained; managed, not native)
  plugin        Discover and inspect out-of-process provider plugins
  sdk           Import and manage user-provided Windows SDK sysroots
  ide           IDE integration helpers
  toolchain     Install and manage cross toolchains under ~/.local/share/lsw/toolchains
  watch         Rebuild automatically when project source files change
  clean         Remove build output (build/, dist/, and the generated resource dir)
  doctor        Diagnose host, runtime, toolchain, and project health
  completions   Generate shell completion scripts (bash, zsh, fish, powershell, elvish)
  man           Generate man pages (top-level to stdout, or all pages into --dir)
  install       Install shell completions and man pages (the binary comes from cargo/your package manager)
  explain       Explain an LSW#### error code
  help          Print this message or the help of the given subcommand(s)

Options:
  -h, --help     Print help (see more with '--help')
  -V, --version  Print version

Global options:
      --verbose          Verbose diagnostic logging
      --trace            Maximum-detail trace logging (implies --verbose)
      --format <FORMAT>  Output format for machine consumption where supported [default: human] [possible values: human, json, csv]
      --env <NAME>       Use this environment instead of the project's active one. Environment-admin commands (ps, kill, registry, service) then work outside a project directory too
      --color <COLOR>    Colored output (default: auto via tty + `NO_COLOR`) [default: auto] [possible values: auto, always, never]
